    "evidence_text": "While successful, this approach is expensive in terms of hardware, runtime and CO_2 emissions.\n\nWhen applying PTLMs to specific domains, it can be useful to domain-adapt them.\n\nDomain adaptation of PTLMs\n\nhas typically been achieved by pretraining on target-domain text.\n\nusually replaced with a randomly initialized task-specific layer.\n\nIn this paper, we focus on BioBERT, as its domain adaptation corpora are publicly available.\n\nIn this paper, we use Word2Vec  to train word vectors.\n\nWe will denote the Word2Vec lookup function as\n\nWe refer to the resulting model as GreenCovidSQuADBERT.\n\ndata and align them with the wordpiece vector space of a general-domain PTLM.\n\nThen, we train CBOW Word2Vec with negative sampling.\n\nFor detailed dataset statistics, see .\n\nLike , we finetune on the concatenation of the training and development set.\n\nAt inference time, we gather the output logits of word-initial wordpieces only.\n\nInexpensive Domain Adaptation of Pretrained Language Models\n\nEvery question is associated with one of 98 research papers (contexts).\n\nWe treat the entire dataset as a test set.\n\nThus, the correct answer may appear several times, often with slightly different wordings.",
